00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.780
So as you've probably
heard this week,

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.180
we've been delighted to
announce that our Healthcare

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:09.420
API has moved to beta.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.060
And what that means is now you
can ingest health care data

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:18.700
into Google Cloud Platform in
its native formats, in FHIR.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.330
Not too many people
yet are using FHIR,

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:24.640
ingesting it
directly in FHIR form

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.340
into Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:31.150
But we're starting to see that
as customers are upgrading

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.720
their EMR versions to
the latest versions that

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.630
support FHIR interfaces.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.780
Most of the data that we
see coming from EHRs today

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.960
comes to us in HL7
version 2 format.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:47.730
And that's part of the Cloud
Healthcare API as well.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:51.340
And in addition, we also
support DICOM images.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:54.820
So any medical radiology
types of image that you have

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:58.540
can also be ingested
natively in this format

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:01.120
directly onto Google
Cloud Platform.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.160
And then what I'm showing
on the right-hand side

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:08.830
is just a simple SQL statement
that's joining EHR and imaging

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.780
data together.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:14.650
And this is the type of
cross modality data analysis

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:18.175
you can do once you've got all
your data together in BigQuery.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:24.310
So that sounds lovely and easy.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:29.040
We all know in practice that
every single data source

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:30.780
is different from the next.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:35.340
And I always like to say
imaging data is nice and large.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.390
It's relatively simple.

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:40.570
EHR data, small and messy.

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:44.250
And so when we think
about harmonizing data,

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:47.400
we think about the
bulk historical data

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.220
that you want to bring
into a cloud CDW, as well

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.470
as the incremental
or streaming sources

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.400
that you're bringing in.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:59.340
And typically, for your bulk
data, those are in CSV formats,

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.410
or it's some kind
of direct connection

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.960
through to an external database.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.040
But for incremental data, as
well as those types of source,

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:12.810
it might also be an HL7 version
2 messages, or FHIR messages,

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:18.070
or other types of
streaming data like that.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:21.600
And what we need to do in
order to harmonize this data

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.050
is think about four key aspects.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.430
Harmonizing the field mappings.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:30.030
So understanding
the source schemas

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:33.280
that you're coming
from, multiple schemas.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.470
What your target schema
is within your cloud CDW.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:40.540
And I'll talk a little bit
about that in a minute.

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:43.140
And then thinking
about how you map

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:47.190
those schemas, the fields
between them, in order

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:51.540
that when you say patient over
here and path ID over there

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:55.500
and PT ID over there, they
all go to the right patient

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:59.250
ID that you've defined
within BigQuery.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:01.860
The next area is
terminology mappings,

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:04.440
because we all know
that particularly

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:08.100
when you acquire a new
hospital into your health

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:10.800
system or a new clinic
into your health system,

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.740
or maybe because you're
trying to integrate data

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:17.820
from lots of different
sources that are messier,

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:21.685
then different sources use
different terminologies.

00:07:21.685 --> 00:07:22.810
Let me give you an example.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:25.920
I've worked with health systems
where some of the medication

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:29.190
data has been in RxNorm,
but other medication data

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:32.550
has been in FDB, because one
particular hospital relies

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:35.670
on FDB as its
medication vocabulary.

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:38.640
And so understanding
how you convert

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:40.920
between these
different terminologies

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:42.570
is also something
that you need to do

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:45.420
as you harmonize this data.

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:48.930
The next area is around
units alignments.

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:51.330
If any of you have
ever visited a NICU,

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:55.830
you'll know that the weight
of babies, of neonate babies,

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.860
is often weighed in
grams, whereas the weight

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.400
of the adult patients in other
ICUs within that same health

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.330
system is typically measured
in kilos if we're lucky,

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.570
or pounds more commonly.

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:12.940
And so aligning the units as
well becomes really important,

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:15.780
not just for things like
math, but also for things

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:18.450
like labs as well.

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:21.480
And then clinical
entity extraction.

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:25.080
I think we've all become very
familiar with physician burnout

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:27.830
and understanding how using--

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:32.580
how physicians like to either
dictate or type up their note.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:36.960
And so extracting out
the richness of that data

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.610
into the structured data,
augmenting the structured data

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:43.409
with the wealth of
information that's

00:08:43.409 --> 00:08:47.070
often buried within the clinical
notes from the providers

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.410
becomes really important.

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:53.250
Now, today, you can do
all of this in Cloud CDW,

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:55.120
but you have to do it manually.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:56.730
And so the tools
that we're working on

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.700
will help to automate
a lot of this,

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:02.040
and over time add
machine learning

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:07.550
to it so that it can start to
suggest the mappings to you.

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:11.370
Over on the right-hand side
here are the target schemas.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:15.950
So just as we recognize health
care data in its native formats

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:21.080
as first class citizens, we
also recognize certain schemas

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:23.210
that are typically
used for health care

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:27.290
analysis as first
class citizens as well.

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:30.050
And starting at the
top there is FHIR.

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:34.340
So if you're looking for a way
to start converting to FHIR,

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:38.820
converting your data to FHIR,
this gives you a path to that.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:42.530
The second one listed here
is OMOP, the OMOP common data

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:45.260
model, which Greg
Klebanov is going to talk

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:47.640
a bit more about in a moment.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:51.650
And then underneath i2b2,
and CDISC, and frankly,

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:55.940
any other schema, because we all
know that many health systems

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:59.000
or for certain analyses,
there are always

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:02.630
schemas that already exist that
people need to continue using,

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:04.830
at least for a while.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:08.600
So you don't need to change
the approach you're taking

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:11.840
in order to leverage Cloud CDW.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:17.150
So right now FHIR and OMOP exist
as target schemas within Cloud

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:20.210
CDW, as well as
the custom schemas.

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:21.900
They exist as well.

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:25.010
And then we'll be adding
CDISC and i2b2 over time.

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:34.030
Of course, you set up your data
harmonization rules, and then

00:10:34.030 --> 00:10:35.350
everything changes.

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:36.370
Your source changes.

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:38.050
Maybe your source
schema changes.

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:39.550
Epic releases a new version.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:41.870
Cerner releases a new version.

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:44.260
Your vocabulary has changed,
because they're always

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:46.270
being updated.

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:48.190
Or your target schema changes.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:49.790
Everything changes
along the way.

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:54.260
And so you need to be
watching for those changes.

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:57.370
And so around this
data harmonization

00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:01.840
and all these transformations
is the framework

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:04.330
to enable you to
monitor and to manage

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:07.360
lifecycle changes within this.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.820
And included within
that, something

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:12.310
that we're starting
to work on now

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:17.770
is understanding when data
is exceptioned, but also what

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:21.820
limits an exception you will
allow through with warnings

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:24.730
versus what things
will really alert you,

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.610
because you need to do
something about that.

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:30.310
And we're leveraging the
concepts of statistical process

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:34.300
control, which is very common
within the manufacturing

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:38.650
industry in order to help
drive that decision making

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:44.320
around when to alert, when to
warn, when to let pass through.

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:47.320
And the lifecycle
management becomes critical,

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:50.500
because from a data provenance
perspective, when you're

00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:53.540
looking to try and
track back the data,

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:57.640
then you also need to understand
where that data came from.

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:01.330
And so understanding
which version of the data

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:04.060
harmonization was in
place at that time

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:07.210
and exactly what that
transformation was, where

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:09.520
the sources of data
were that came,

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:11.200
that becomes really important.

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:16.030
I mentioned the public
data sets before.

00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:20.430
We now have over 100 public
data sets available on BigQuery

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:23.490
that you can just integrate
into any analysis,

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:28.235
join as tables into any analysis
that you are doing on BigQuery.

00:12:28.235 --> 00:12:29.610
These are some of
the health care

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:32.070
and life science ones available.

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:34.350
There's a bunch of
biomedical data sets

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:38.220
that we're bringing on as part
of the NIH strides initiative.

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:42.880
And then there's a fair bit
of Medicare and Medicaid

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:45.240
data from CMS.

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:47.220
But we're also
always in the process

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:48.630
of adding more data sets.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:50.730
And right now my focus
is trying to ensure

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:53.810
that we add more social
determinants of health data

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:56.370
sets that will help
with the analysis

00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:59.580
that you're trying to do
within your populations.

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:07.340
Once you've harmonized
all that data,

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:09.090
what do you want to do with it?

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:12.230
Maybe you just want to create
some kind of dashboard,

00:13:12.230 --> 00:13:15.800
some kinds of analytics that
will help you understand things

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:18.740
like the longitudinal
patient record,

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:23.850
or maybe you need to create
analytics that are portable.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:28.430
So I think about defining
cohorts of patients

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:32.090
in the same way that
I think about needing

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:36.950
to enable clinicians to do those
definitions in terms that they

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:38.420
understand.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:41.765
So not looking at the
detailed fields and saying,

00:13:41.765 --> 00:13:45.710
ah, I want to select
on this field,

00:13:45.710 --> 00:13:48.680
and I need to join it with
that thing over there.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:52.460
But rather enabling a user
interface that clinicians

00:13:52.460 --> 00:13:55.890
can say, look, these are
my inclusion criteria

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:58.370
and these are my
exclusion criteria,

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:02.390
and be able to leverage
common definitions for things

00:14:02.390 --> 00:14:05.540
like certain
conditions, leveraging

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:07.970
clinical quality
metric definitions,

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:10.190
leveraging other
common definitions

00:14:10.190 --> 00:14:13.740
that are out there
in our industry.

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:19.040
And so you'll be able to
create your cohort definitions,

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:21.740
and underneath these
will be made portable

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:26.600
so that you can then apply those
cohorts definitions to any data

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:30.560
set that could be
enabled within FHIR.

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:33.200
So this becomes really
powerful for you,

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:36.950
because it helps to
protect your investment.

00:14:36.950 --> 00:14:38.780
As well as cohort
definitions, you

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:42.530
can think of query definitions
and metric definitions

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:44.330
in the same way.

00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:46.820
So we'll be couching
behind the scenes

00:14:46.820 --> 00:14:49.760
these definitions within FHIR.

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:52.490
And of course, we'll
help you get to FHIR.

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:56.360
So that that way, if you
bring on another data set,

00:14:56.360 --> 00:14:59.060
all you need to do is
realign it to FHIR,

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:01.550
and then apply that
definition on top of it.

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:03.290
And that could be a query.

00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:04.790
That could be a metric.

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:07.190
That could be a cohort.

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:11.450
And over time, we'll also enable
you to share these definitions

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:14.130
with others as well.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:16.970
And so that too
helps, particularly

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:19.250
in the research
world, particularly

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:23.122
as you're looking at doing
more collaboration with others

00:15:23.122 --> 00:15:23.705
in your field.

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:31.580
Another reason why people
want to bring all of this data

00:15:31.580 --> 00:15:34.250
together-- and I've spent quite
a bit of time this week talking

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:37.160
to several people about this--

00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:39.170
is in order to use it
for machine learning,

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:42.260
in order to use it
either to train a model

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:46.700
or, more often right now,
to be able to infer data,

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:51.110
to be able to predict data
using somebody else's model.

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:53.180
Well, we'll meet
you where you are.

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:58.070
We have a range of different
machine learning tools,

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:02.810
APIs, and techniques
that can help you.

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:06.410
And all of these work
directly on top of BigQuery

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:11.690
and on top of our Cloud CDW.

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:17.180
Over on your left, are the ones
that require more engineering.

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:20.450
So things like TensorFlow or
being able to leverage Spark

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:23.210
ML, Cloud CMLE.

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:25.820
In the middle are some
of the predefined models

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:27.480
that we have available.

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:30.260
So for example, our
Natural Language API,

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:35.910
our Video Intelligence API,
and also our Translation API.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:38.990
And then over on the
right, BigQuery ML,

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:44.120
which so far is primarily for
classification and regressions,

00:16:44.120 --> 00:16:48.870
but over time may expand as
well in its capabilities.

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:53.790
So if you want to train machine
learning models, bring it on.

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:56.540
Bring it into
Cloud CDW, and then

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:58.730
have all of these
different tools

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:01.130
available for you to do that.

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:05.359
Now, if you're trying to apply
somebody else's models on top

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:09.829
of the data in Cloud CDW,
we'll help you do that, too.

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:13.130
So our framework will let you
invoke other machine learning

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:16.204
APIs as well to make
those predictions.

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:20.980
Finally, you've done that.

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:23.040
And in some cases,
that's it, right?

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:26.740
You're using that
data internally.

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:30.180
In other cases, particularly
in some research cases,

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:33.060
you'll want to deidentify
that data in order

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:35.880
to make it available
to researchers.

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:39.300
And so that the
Deidentification API

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:43.660
is the first step on the way to
being able to share those data

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:46.290
sets more broadly with
other researchers.

00:17:46.290 --> 00:17:49.060
We have other things in
the works there as well,

00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:52.080
but this is what's
available right now.

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:56.610
And this Deidentification works
not just on structured data.

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:58.530
That's relatively easy.

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:00.790
But also on imaging data.

00:18:00.790 --> 00:18:04.350
So if there is PHI burned
into the medical image

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:06.810
pixels, which there often is.

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:09.690
And it also works on
unstructured clinical notes

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:11.220
as well.

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:13.750
And in addition, on
the structured data,

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:16.860
if you already have
some deidentified data,

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:20.580
we can now present back to you
the risk of reidentification

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:24.870
of that data as well so that
you can understand where

00:18:24.870 --> 00:18:26.460
the risk lies on the spectrum.

00:18:31.270 --> 00:18:33.910
I talked a little bit about
this actually last year

00:18:33.910 --> 00:18:38.740
when I said I think we'd run
three datathons at that point

00:18:38.740 --> 00:18:43.690
on GCP and had, I think, another
six coming up in the next year.

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:45.400
Well, that proliferated.

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:46.870
They bred like bunnies.

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:50.090
And we've actually supported
now 11 over the last year.

00:18:50.090 --> 00:18:54.460
These are a great way to get
your team's direct experience

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:57.390
of using GCP with
health care data.

00:18:57.390 --> 00:18:59.590
They're held around the world.

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:01.960
If anybody would like
more information,

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:04.250
come up and see us at the end.

00:19:04.250 --> 00:19:06.730
But this is essentially
a little package

00:19:06.730 --> 00:19:10.330
that allows organizers
to host health care

00:19:10.330 --> 00:19:15.770
datathon in an appropriate
environment for that.

00:19:15.770 --> 00:19:19.450
And so if you want to host a
datathon, come up and see us.

00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:21.370
Just bring your data
set to us, and we're

00:19:21.370 --> 00:19:25.390
happy to help get
that set up with you.

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:28.930
Or if you are interested
in attending them or having

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:32.780
one of your team attend
one, let us know as well.

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:35.560
Typically, what happens is
over the course of a weekend,

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:38.800
everybody gets together
and really blacks

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:43.570
out clinical data science
on top of BigQuery

00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:48.080
and on top using
data science tools.

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:49.750
So with that, I'd
like to hand over

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:53.500
to Josh, who's going to talk
about on the data ingestion

00:19:53.500 --> 00:20:01.080
process how you take that
data, X12 data in this case,

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:02.820
and convert it into FHIR.

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:05.047
And with Josh is Alen as well.

00:20:05.047 --> 00:20:06.435
Thank you.

00:20:06.435 --> 00:20:07.810
JOSH SIEGEL: Thank
you, Marianne.

00:20:07.810 --> 00:20:10.240
Is everybody excited
to take a look

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:14.950
at a deep dive at the clinical
data hiding in X12 ANSI format?

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:16.450
[CHEERING]

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:17.380
Yes.

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:18.190
All right.

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:24.340
So as some of you may know, Care
Cloud is an electronic practice

00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:28.780
management and electronic
medical records system platform

00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:31.030
entirely hosted in the cloud.

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:33.190
And one of the reasons
why we're here with you

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:36.130
guys today is we've been a
technology partner working

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.920
with the GCP team,
particularly on the Healthcare

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:41.380
API for the better
part of the last year.

00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:43.888
So going through the
various alpha phases.

00:20:43.888 --> 00:20:45.430
And one of the things
that we've been

00:20:45.430 --> 00:20:47.347
able to do that we want
to demonstrate for you

00:20:47.347 --> 00:20:51.760
today, as Marianne mentioned,
all of the great power

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:54.760
and insight that you can
gain from your data having it

00:20:54.760 --> 00:20:58.510
formatted in FHIR and having
it prepared for prediction,

00:20:58.510 --> 00:21:01.270
this is an example of
how that can be done,

00:21:01.270 --> 00:21:03.850
and frankly, how easy
it looks once you've

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:08.840
taken the time to build that
out in your own systems.

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:13.580
So how many people in
here are familiar with X12

00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:15.440
and have dealt with
that as a data format?

00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:16.860
Show of hands.

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:17.360
OK.

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:18.193
Not too many people.

00:21:18.193 --> 00:21:20.750
We'll dive into
what it's used for,

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:24.440
but primarily, this is the
way that electronic practice

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:29.270
management systems, medical
records systems, big pharmacy

00:21:29.270 --> 00:21:33.620
benefit management companies
transact between each other.

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:36.050
This is a 40-year-old
interoperability format.

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:37.670
We were both born in 1979.

00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:40.190
That's why I like X12.

00:21:40.190 --> 00:21:43.220
But it's in use
quite prevalent early

00:21:43.220 --> 00:21:45.780
across all of the big payers.

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:48.170
And what that means
is a lot of the data

00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:51.290
that we want to
analyze longitudinally,

00:21:51.290 --> 00:21:53.780
especially when you think
about the clinical perspective,

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:58.310
is locked up in these really
old archival data formats.

00:21:58.310 --> 00:22:00.920
So FHIR coming around,
and now with FHIR

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:04.790
v4, we're standardizing
on a normative model that

00:22:04.790 --> 00:22:07.820
is going to allow us
to essentially continue

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:10.280
with backward compatibility,
have this normative format

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:12.680
that we can do analysis on.

00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:15.020
So Care Cloud,
because of the way

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:18.650
that we service our
existing provider customers,

00:22:18.650 --> 00:22:23.036
already has invested a lot
of technology and know-how

00:22:23.036 --> 00:22:27.670
into translating very
specific, detailed data,

00:22:27.670 --> 00:22:30.260
in this case bidirectionally.

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:34.730
So we're talking about fully
interoperable, bidirectional

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:37.190
semantic translation
of data from

00:22:37.190 --> 00:22:39.530
the different-- the
various components of X12

00:22:39.530 --> 00:22:42.740
messages into the
equivalent FHIR resource.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:44.480
That's a mouthful,
so we're going

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:47.840
to actually pop into a
demonstration to kind of show

00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:51.550
how easy it is once it's
been put into practice.

00:22:51.550 --> 00:22:55.785
Alen Pulido here is our
technical lead whose team

00:22:55.785 --> 00:22:56.910
did all of the development.

00:22:56.910 --> 00:23:00.560
So he's up here
making it happen.

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:02.690
So this is our console.

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:05.780
We're announcing here
at the conference

00:23:05.780 --> 00:23:09.525
the beta of this as part of the
beta for the Healthcare API.

00:23:09.525 --> 00:23:11.900
So this is going to be available
through the marketplace,

00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:13.790
or working with us,
we can offer this

00:23:13.790 --> 00:23:17.180
as a managed service
or a SaaS offering.

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:20.660
But we're going to go through
how simple the configuration is

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:26.840
to go from an X12 archive into
FHIR resources in the Google

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:28.160
Healthcare API.

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:29.730
So we're going to start off.

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:32.600
We're going to go through
and configure a tenant.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:35.240
And if you think about
the various different ways

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:38.250
that X12 can be managed
inside an organization,

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:41.450
you might have dozens or
hundreds of different X12

00:23:41.450 --> 00:23:45.290
definitions for a single
message type in a large pay

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:46.410
organization.

00:23:46.410 --> 00:23:50.270
So in this case,
we're going to say

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:53.210
we have options for the
sources during the beta.

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:55.070
GCS is obviously our favorite.

00:23:55.070 --> 00:23:58.580
But if you're looking to move
into the Google Healthcare API

00:23:58.580 --> 00:24:03.080
from somebody else, those
are available as well.

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:05.810
If we pop over, it's
as simple as basically

00:24:05.810 --> 00:24:08.960
configuring the bucket that you
want to pull the data out of

00:24:08.960 --> 00:24:11.630
and targeting your FHIR server.

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:14.460
In this case, it's the
Google Healthcare API.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:17.030
I'll do another quick
survey before we

00:24:17.030 --> 00:24:18.440
kick this next part off.

00:24:18.440 --> 00:24:21.980
How many of you have
your own FHIR servers

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:23.810
or have a team of
engineers back home

00:24:23.810 --> 00:24:28.090
that are trying to build
your own FHIR solution?

00:24:28.090 --> 00:24:29.635
OK.

00:24:29.635 --> 00:24:31.760
Even if you're not, go back
home and tell everybody

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:34.570
to stop doing that.

00:24:34.570 --> 00:24:37.743
I'll testify for a second
from just personal experience.

00:24:37.743 --> 00:24:39.410
We went through a lot
of the open source

00:24:39.410 --> 00:24:43.010
projects, evaluated
nearly all of them,

00:24:43.010 --> 00:24:46.040
went so far as to even look
at developing our own cloud

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:47.870
hosted FHIR server in-house.

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:51.080
Really, when you look at the
amount of raw engineering

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:53.620
that it takes to get
that all the way through,

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:56.900
take advantage of the
work that Kurt is doing.

00:24:56.900 --> 00:24:58.330
That team is really strong.

00:24:58.330 --> 00:25:01.010
They're really committed to
maintaining the standard.

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:04.130
And frankly, the service
is really performant.

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:07.250
As we've gotten through the
various phases of the alpha,

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:10.265
it's really at a place
where it's enterprise

00:25:10.265 --> 00:25:11.390
grade, production strength.

00:25:11.390 --> 00:25:14.580
You can really pump a
lot of data through it.

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:17.420
So we're going to
go ahead and trigger

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:22.820
a sweep of all of the,
in this case, 837 claims.

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:26.450
So, well, we talked
about how many

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:27.990
people had ever seen an 837.

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:30.170
So we're going to give you
a sneak peek at what that

00:25:30.170 --> 00:25:31.710
actually looks like.

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:35.060
So if you're a COBOL programmer,
if you like mainframes,

00:25:35.060 --> 00:25:38.210
this is what we love.

00:25:38.210 --> 00:25:43.490
But locked in here is all of
the valuable procedures that

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:47.120
were performed on the patient,
the related diagnoses that

00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:49.640
were used as the supporting
clinical rationale for why

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:52.070
that procedure
was performed, all

00:25:52.070 --> 00:25:54.320
of the patient
detailed demographics.

00:25:54.320 --> 00:25:59.120
It's a very good dense
data format 40 years ago

00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:01.413
when you really needed to
transact between mainframes.

00:26:01.413 --> 00:26:03.080
And frankly, there's
billions of dollars

00:26:03.080 --> 00:26:05.060
of existing
investment in keeping

00:26:05.060 --> 00:26:08.060
this format operational.

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:10.400
And this is, frankly,
the backbone of the way

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:14.240
that we process medical
claims in the US.

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:18.740
So if we go back,
again, thinking

00:26:18.740 --> 00:26:21.290
about the power of
the way GCP allows

00:26:21.290 --> 00:26:22.910
us to build data
pipelines and do

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:24.860
these types of
transformations, we

00:26:24.860 --> 00:26:28.460
can do this at batch or
in a streaming fashion.

00:26:28.460 --> 00:26:31.195
So as these X12 messages
are being generated,

00:26:31.195 --> 00:26:32.570
we can translate
them on the fly.

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:36.410
Now, if you flip--

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:37.830
roll this forward.

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:41.720
Now, as more and more of
our industry is trend--

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:45.590
standardizing on FHIR as
the primary data format,

00:26:45.590 --> 00:26:47.300
this type of
semantic translation

00:26:47.300 --> 00:26:49.850
allows us to take
the FHIR resource

00:26:49.850 --> 00:26:52.550
and translate it
now back into X12,

00:26:52.550 --> 00:26:55.310
which is the format that
your mainframes are typically

00:26:55.310 --> 00:26:56.930
going to be processing.

00:26:56.930 --> 00:26:58.200
So we can pop in.

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:02.630
And we call it a human
readable X12 message.

00:27:02.630 --> 00:27:04.370
And that means
that, really, you're

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:08.600
an analyst that specializes in
looking at these types of data.

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:12.230
But breaking it out by the
various loops and segments,

00:27:12.230 --> 00:27:14.900
you can see the nested
hierarchy the way

00:27:14.900 --> 00:27:16.750
this data gets generated.

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:19.250
And again, we're talking about
that full fidelity capability

00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:22.280
of translating it on the wire.

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:23.660
We have another legacy format.

00:27:23.660 --> 00:27:28.010
If you guys are familiar with
this, this is a CMS-1500.

00:27:28.010 --> 00:27:29.900
But again, you think
about the power

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:32.870
of being able to have this
type of semantic mapping.

00:27:32.870 --> 00:27:36.170
You can just talk about
processing images.

00:27:36.170 --> 00:27:39.080
We can generate the
CMS-1500 claim form directly

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:43.090
from what would have been an
electronic claims submission.

00:27:43.090 --> 00:27:45.990
Now, the real power
and where this

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:47.730
flows into the
clinical data analysis

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:50.880
is being able to get into
the FHIR resource itself.

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:58.430
So if you're thinking about
modern development languages,

00:27:58.430 --> 00:28:01.220
modern programming
techniques, most folks

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:04.340
are not going to be
building those applications

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:07.370
and those capabilities against
that native X12 format.

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:08.708
But there is a semantic mapping.

00:28:08.708 --> 00:28:10.250
And there is a way
of representing it

00:28:10.250 --> 00:28:11.990
as a FHIR resource.

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:16.052
In this case, we can go down
and take a look at the--

00:28:16.052 --> 00:28:17.510
we'll take a look
at the diagnosis,

00:28:17.510 --> 00:28:19.520
or do you want to look
at the-- we've got that.

00:28:19.520 --> 00:28:20.180
OK.

00:28:20.180 --> 00:28:21.470
The claim ID.

00:28:21.470 --> 00:28:23.720
So again, we're talking
about bringing this

00:28:23.720 --> 00:28:29.120
from a legacy or an
archival claim storage.

00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:33.260
FHIR is really good at being
able to identify resources

00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:34.557
across systems.

00:28:34.557 --> 00:28:36.890
And if you're bringing something
out of a legacy system,

00:28:36.890 --> 00:28:39.950
maintaining the identity
integrity from the system that

00:28:39.950 --> 00:28:42.410
generated it, and as you move
it out to the FHIR server

00:28:42.410 --> 00:28:46.340
being able to reference it
without having to copy it.

00:28:46.340 --> 00:28:49.220
And for all of us
here, as true believers

00:28:49.220 --> 00:28:51.990
in clinical and clinical
data analysis and health

00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:56.060
care IT in general, this allows
us, as Marianne mentioned,

00:28:56.060 --> 00:29:00.320
as we're publishing data sets,
as we're evolving our ability

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:02.630
to make this data
useful and available,

00:29:02.630 --> 00:29:06.740
we can leave that data in the
resident FHIR server, where

00:29:06.740 --> 00:29:10.190
the entity that's responsible
for managing it is,

00:29:10.190 --> 00:29:13.280
and we can reference
it through FHIR.

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:15.260
And so that's really
our goal here.

00:29:15.260 --> 00:29:19.560
It's to maintain that
identity and integrity.

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:25.280
So the other great part is now,
if you think about the way EMRs

00:29:25.280 --> 00:29:27.740
and everybody are building
the supporting information

00:29:27.740 --> 00:29:30.590
clinically, that all
gets represented in FHIR

00:29:30.590 --> 00:29:33.560
as a procedure code
and a diagnosis code.

00:29:33.560 --> 00:29:36.080
And they're mapped here so
that you have the linked

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:37.850
diagnosis with all
of the procedures

00:29:37.850 --> 00:29:40.300
that were performed
on the patient.

00:29:40.300 --> 00:29:42.680
And now, in our
case, I know we've

00:29:42.680 --> 00:29:46.280
mentioned a few other EMRs,
but some EMRs out there

00:29:46.280 --> 00:29:48.680
are generating
their clinical data

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:51.200
in a FHIR compatible format.

00:29:51.200 --> 00:29:55.010
So as these things
evolve, this is really

00:29:55.010 --> 00:30:00.050
where we can start to find
out things totally new.

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:03.303
Maybe there's a
propensity to not

00:30:03.303 --> 00:30:05.720
go to your follow-up appointment
after your last claim was

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:06.590
denied.

00:30:06.590 --> 00:30:10.250
Now, if you're putting
these data together,

00:30:10.250 --> 00:30:12.470
you can start to map out
how patients were treated

00:30:12.470 --> 00:30:15.382
against how the financial
information was actually

00:30:15.382 --> 00:30:17.840
transacted between the patient
and their insurance company.

