the context of OHDSI

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:13.690
as an open-science community.

00:34:13.690 --> 00:34:15.679
So OHDSI is
pronounced as Odyssey.

00:34:15.679 --> 00:34:18.580
It's spelled O-H-D-S-I. It
stands for Observational Health

00:34:18.580 --> 00:34:20.080
Data Sciences and Informatics.

00:34:20.080 --> 00:34:25.150
It's, again, an international
open-science, open-source

00:34:25.150 --> 00:34:30.969
community that is
focused on enabling

00:34:30.969 --> 00:34:35.469
standardized, reproducible,
scalable, observational data

00:34:35.469 --> 00:34:37.540
analytics across the world.

00:34:37.540 --> 00:34:40.679
And what I'd like to
stress is that OHDSI

00:34:40.679 --> 00:34:43.989
is an open-source, open-science
community, meaning it's public.

00:34:43.989 --> 00:34:44.739
It's open.

00:34:44.739 --> 00:34:45.850
It's international.

00:34:45.850 --> 00:34:47.560
And anybody can join.

00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:48.880
Anybody can contribute.

00:34:48.880 --> 00:34:52.330
And anybody can use any of the
tools and standards and methods

00:34:52.330 --> 00:34:56.409
that are developed
by this community.

00:34:56.409 --> 00:35:00.820
It's worth mentioning that
Columbia University is

00:35:00.820 --> 00:35:04.270
a coordinating center
across the world.

00:35:04.270 --> 00:35:06.740
As you can see, OHDSI
is a global community.

00:35:06.740 --> 00:35:10.130
It spans multiple countries,
multiple continents,

00:35:10.130 --> 00:35:12.840
and today you can
see the statistics.

00:35:12.840 --> 00:35:14.810
It's growing quite nicely.

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:19.390
And we work very closely with
our partners and collaborators

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:22.620
in the US, in
Europe, and in Asia,

00:35:22.620 --> 00:35:25.980
including South Korea and China.

00:35:25.980 --> 00:35:29.120
So the mission of OHDSI is to
really improve public health

00:35:29.120 --> 00:35:32.900
by enabling better health
care decisions based

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:37.870
on evidence produced from
real world data sets.

00:35:37.870 --> 00:35:41.280
And so one of the--

00:35:41.280 --> 00:35:42.460
apologies for that.

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:45.250
So OHDSI, again, is an
open-science, open-source

00:35:45.250 --> 00:35:50.470
community that enables key
open standards, including

00:35:50.470 --> 00:35:53.170
the standard that's
called OMOP CDM.

00:35:53.170 --> 00:35:55.610
Common Data Model, that's
what CDM stands for.

00:35:55.610 --> 00:35:57.140
And the latest
version, version 6--

00:35:57.140 --> 00:35:58.390
you can see it on the screen--

00:35:58.390 --> 00:36:01.040
is quite mature, quite
nicely developed.

00:36:01.040 --> 00:36:04.240
So in addition to a
common data model,

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:07.240
OHDSI also enabled a set
of standardized medical

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:09.640
vocabularies and a set
of standardized tools.

00:36:09.640 --> 00:36:11.670
And one of those tools is ATLAS.

00:36:11.670 --> 00:36:14.870
And I'll show it to you today.

00:36:14.870 --> 00:36:16.840
But before I dive
into ATLAS, I'd

00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:19.570
just like to maybe talk a
little bit about the data

00:36:19.570 --> 00:36:22.810
journey of how this data gets
into data analytics platforms

00:36:22.810 --> 00:36:24.700
like ATLAS.

00:36:24.700 --> 00:36:27.190
So data sets, what kind of
data sets are we working with?

00:36:27.190 --> 00:36:29.050
We're working with
clinical data sets,

00:36:29.050 --> 00:36:31.390
or what we call
real world data that

00:36:31.390 --> 00:36:34.950
comes from different types of
organizations that come from--

00:36:34.950 --> 00:36:37.150
well, they could be
medical insurance claims,

00:36:37.150 --> 00:36:39.290
or electronic medical records.

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:44.050
They could be a lab results,
survey data, clinical trial

00:36:44.050 --> 00:36:46.640
data, chart reviews, and so on.

00:36:46.640 --> 00:36:49.120
So any types of
data that is coming

00:36:49.120 --> 00:36:51.970
from a clinical utilization
of drugs and treatments,

00:36:51.970 --> 00:36:57.300
we essentially need
to process and convert

00:36:57.300 --> 00:36:59.740
into a standardized
format called OMOP CDM.

00:36:59.740 --> 00:37:01.930
So what's nice
about OMOP CDM, what

00:37:01.930 --> 00:37:04.630
creates a single standardized
way of saving data,

00:37:04.630 --> 00:37:08.170
so that data scientists
or researchers only

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:10.390
have to design their
query once, and then they

00:37:10.390 --> 00:37:13.870
can go and see that this query
is going to run on any OMOP CDM

00:37:13.870 --> 00:37:16.270
database across the world.

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:19.980
And I'm really excited
to say that we added--

00:37:19.980 --> 00:37:22.650
so ATLAS, and many
other tools in OHDSI,

00:37:22.650 --> 00:37:25.880
they support, I believe, close
to nine different data sets--

00:37:25.880 --> 00:37:27.660
I'm sorry-- databases.

00:37:27.660 --> 00:37:30.390
And I'm really excited
to say that we also

00:37:30.390 --> 00:37:36.703
now provide support for GCP
platform, including big data--

00:37:36.703 --> 00:37:37.620
BigQuery-- I'm sorry--

00:37:37.620 --> 00:37:40.210
BigQuery database.

00:37:40.210 --> 00:37:43.490
So what you see on the
screen is essentially

00:37:43.490 --> 00:37:47.580
a simplified architecture
of how we process

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:50.490
data to convert into OMOP CDM.

00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:54.480
And we've successfully
converted data sets,

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:57.090
actually pretty large
data sets utilizing

00:37:57.090 --> 00:38:00.360
Dataproc and Apache Spark.

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:03.150
So it's also worth
mentioning that the data sets

00:38:03.150 --> 00:38:05.710
that we're working with are
very, very large in size.

00:38:05.710 --> 00:38:07.150
They can be very, very large.

00:38:07.150 --> 00:38:11.730
Medical claims data tend to
grow in data aggregators,

00:38:11.730 --> 00:38:14.860
such as IBM MarketScan
Databases tend

00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:18.980
to be over 200 million patients
in size for a single database.

00:38:18.980 --> 00:38:22.620
So it would typically
mean tens of billions

00:38:22.620 --> 00:38:24.840
of records that need to
be processed and converted

00:38:24.840 --> 00:38:27.970
into OMOP CDM.

00:38:27.970 --> 00:38:32.800
I'm also very excited to say
that OHDSI ATLAS, which is--

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:34.420
oh, I don't know
what happened here.

00:38:34.420 --> 00:38:36.520
OHDSI ATLAS, that
is probably one

00:38:36.520 --> 00:38:40.830
of the most common and popular
tools in the OHDSI community,

00:38:40.830 --> 00:38:44.140
also now supports BigQuery
database underneath.

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:48.160
And I'll give you a little demo
of that tool in the second.

00:38:48.160 --> 00:38:51.280
So ATLAS is a tool that
was produced and is

00:38:51.280 --> 00:38:53.300
produced and developed
by the OHDSI community.

00:38:53.300 --> 00:38:55.690
So my company,
Odysseus Data Services,

00:38:55.690 --> 00:38:59.530
is one of the most
active contributors

00:38:59.530 --> 00:39:01.300
in developing this tool.

00:39:01.300 --> 00:39:03.730
And ATLAS is a tool
that was designed

00:39:03.730 --> 00:39:05.380
to enable research
of data scientists

00:39:05.380 --> 00:39:09.850
with an ability to design
complex analysis studies,

00:39:09.850 --> 00:39:13.750
and then execute those studies
on the top of the OMOP CDM

00:39:13.750 --> 00:39:14.648
database.

00:39:22.470 --> 00:39:24.050
I need some magic here.

00:39:24.050 --> 00:39:24.800
Great.

00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:27.995
So bear with me one second.

00:39:27.995 --> 00:39:29.620
So what you see right
now on the screen

00:39:29.620 --> 00:39:32.600
is the latest release
of OHDSI ATLAS tool.

00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:35.630
And the tool is actually
quite mature at this point.

00:39:35.630 --> 00:39:37.370
It's quite sophisticated.

00:39:37.370 --> 00:39:42.000
It offers multiple
quiet nice features,

00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:45.710
including a full database
level characteristics,

00:39:45.710 --> 00:39:47.550
which you will find like--

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:49.340
this thing is on.

00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:51.050
I don't see the mouse.

00:39:51.050 --> 00:39:51.550
Here we go.

00:39:54.310 --> 00:39:57.190
It's called ACHILLES.

00:39:57.190 --> 00:40:01.650
So ACHILLES is a full database
level characterization

00:40:01.650 --> 00:40:02.510
of your data set.

00:40:02.510 --> 00:40:06.000
So you can imagine that
before a scientist even

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:09.600
begins to design their
study and research,

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:12.410
they would like to know what's
available in a database.

00:40:12.410 --> 00:40:16.280
And this information should
be coming from ACHILLES.

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:18.600
So ACHILLES allows
you as a researcher

00:40:18.600 --> 00:40:23.130
to see data stratified into
different types of dimensions

00:40:23.130 --> 00:40:27.050
that are available in
OMOP CDM, including it

00:40:27.050 --> 00:40:29.250
will give you dashboard
information, demographics

00:40:29.250 --> 00:40:33.180
information, visit information,
conditions information,

00:40:33.180 --> 00:40:36.550
procedures, drug exposures,
measurements, and so on.

00:40:36.550 --> 00:40:37.800
So it's for any researcher.

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:40.620
It's very, very, very
easy to come to ACHILLES

00:40:40.620 --> 00:40:44.220
and just look up if this
specific database contains

00:40:44.220 --> 00:40:46.860
information about
a condition/disease

00:40:46.860 --> 00:40:48.990
or a drug of interest
before they even run

00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:52.230
a complex analytics encoding.

00:40:52.230 --> 00:40:55.110
So another secret
sauce in OMOP CDM

00:40:55.110 --> 00:40:57.660
is a set of standardized
medical vocabulary.

00:40:57.660 --> 00:41:00.750
As Marianne
previously said, drugs

00:41:00.750 --> 00:41:03.040
can be quoted in
many different ways.

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:07.635
And different drug norms
exist, and OCD, the vocabulary

00:41:07.635 --> 00:41:08.610
of this.

00:41:08.610 --> 00:41:11.340
So one of the most
fundamental standards

00:41:11.340 --> 00:41:13.230
and decisions that
OHDSI made is that they

00:41:13.230 --> 00:41:14.790
decided to standardize.

00:41:14.790 --> 00:41:16.810
So what our team
is actually doing,

00:41:16.810 --> 00:41:19.200
they bring together multiple
medical vocabularies,

00:41:19.200 --> 00:41:23.190
and they standardize on a
single representation of a drug,

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:25.620
on a single representation
of a condition,

00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:28.620
so that when data is being
converted from raw data

00:41:28.620 --> 00:41:31.860
into OMOP CDM, data
is being converted

00:41:31.860 --> 00:41:33.750
into a standardized
representation

00:41:33.750 --> 00:41:35.850
of that condition or a drug.

00:41:35.850 --> 00:41:38.760
So here, in ATLAS, you'll
be able, as a researcher,

00:41:38.760 --> 00:41:42.450
to search and browse
medical vocabularies.

00:41:42.450 --> 00:41:43.230
So cohorts.

00:41:43.230 --> 00:41:53.940
Any mature analytical tool
offers today a cohort builder,

00:41:53.940 --> 00:41:55.460
a cohort designer of some sort.

00:41:55.460 --> 00:41:58.860
And so ATLAS is probably one
of the most sophisticated tools

00:41:58.860 --> 00:42:02.140
that has been developed
in the industry.

00:42:02.140 --> 00:42:05.520
So it allows you as a researcher
to design very sophisticated

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:07.190
cohort information.

00:42:07.190 --> 00:42:08.780
Not only you can
design a cohort--

00:42:08.780 --> 00:42:10.800
and I've got some
samples there I'm

00:42:10.800 --> 00:42:12.340
going to show you in a second--

00:42:12.340 --> 00:42:13.830
but you can also--

00:42:13.830 --> 00:42:15.960
as you design your
cohorts, you obviously

00:42:15.960 --> 00:42:18.750
take advantage of standardized
medical vocabularies,

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:22.960
and then you can execute some
basic statistics immediately

00:42:22.960 --> 00:42:24.300
by utilizing this cohort.

00:42:24.300 --> 00:42:29.670
So for example, I've
generated some accounts.

00:42:29.670 --> 00:42:32.230
I designed a cohort that
is meaningful to me.

00:42:32.230 --> 00:42:35.790
So it's ibuprofen being taken
by a male over 40 years old.

00:42:35.790 --> 00:42:38.880
And I ran it against
the CMS data set.

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:40.506
So SynPUF is a
synthetic data set.

00:42:40.506 --> 00:42:44.220
It's a representation of
CMS data, claims data.

00:42:44.220 --> 00:42:47.130
And I can immediately see
that my cohort resulted

00:42:47.130 --> 00:42:52.230
in 170,000 patients that
would fit the cohort.

00:42:52.230 --> 00:42:56.240
And I immediately see how
my filters, my criteria

00:42:56.240 --> 00:42:58.950
limited a cohort
selection at the end.

00:42:58.950 --> 00:43:01.560
So cohorts are the building
blocks in any data analytics

00:43:01.560 --> 00:43:02.830
tool.

00:43:02.830 --> 00:43:05.520
And again, the user can see this
is running against a BigQuery

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:07.290
database.

00:43:07.290 --> 00:43:08.950
And then it gets
more complicated.

00:43:08.950 --> 00:43:15.690
Of course, as scientists
continue their research,

00:43:15.690 --> 00:43:19.530
they would like to know
not only how many patients

00:43:19.530 --> 00:43:22.410
do exist in a specific
data set, but they

00:43:22.410 --> 00:43:24.780
would like to get a
full characterization

00:43:24.780 --> 00:43:26.370
of that cohort.

00:43:26.370 --> 00:43:28.560
Characterization
meaning take this cohort

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:32.940
and stratify it by different
dimensions, by different--

00:43:32.940 --> 00:43:36.060
that exist in the
OMOP CDM data set.

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:38.475
So for example, in this
case, this specific case,

00:43:38.475 --> 00:43:40.190
I actually took two cohorts--

00:43:40.190 --> 00:43:43.020
one aspirin users and one
ibuprofen users-- and I

00:43:43.020 --> 00:43:46.470
put them against one another.

00:43:46.470 --> 00:43:48.958
For some reason I
can't scroll again.

00:43:48.958 --> 00:43:51.440
Here we go.

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:54.830
So as you can see, I can
now characterize my cohort

00:43:54.830 --> 00:44:02.450
by different criterias that I'm
able as a researcher to select.

00:44:02.450 --> 00:44:04.580
Then as one of the
latest features

00:44:04.580 --> 00:44:09.100
that was added in ATLAS
is the treatment pathway.

00:44:09.100 --> 00:44:11.950
So especially--
treatment pathways

00:44:11.950 --> 00:44:15.632
are very important when, for
example, an oncology patient

00:44:15.632 --> 00:44:16.840
is going through a treatment.

00:44:16.840 --> 00:44:19.727
And it's very often when
oncology patient is going

00:44:19.727 --> 00:44:22.060
through a treatment, they go
through a multiple specific

00:44:22.060 --> 00:44:24.200
predefined treatment pathway.

00:44:24.200 --> 00:44:25.915
So in this specific
case, actually, it's

00:44:25.915 --> 00:44:27.040
not talking about oncology.

00:44:27.040 --> 00:44:29.120
It's talking about diabetes 2.

00:44:29.120 --> 00:44:31.000
But you can see a
pathway of treatment

00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:34.990
of different patients, of
how they go from drug one

00:44:34.990 --> 00:44:39.970
into drug two and to drug
three throughout their journey.

00:44:39.970 --> 00:44:41.008
Incident rates.

00:44:41.008 --> 00:44:43.300
Basically incident rates
provide exactly what it means.

00:44:43.300 --> 00:44:49.450
It's the incident
of certain outcomes

00:44:49.450 --> 00:44:54.710
in the set of cohort patients.

00:44:54.710 --> 00:44:58.970
So in this specific
case, it's incidence of--

00:44:58.970 --> 00:45:00.980
I'm not sure what that means.

00:45:00.980 --> 00:45:02.210
I'm sorry.

00:45:02.210 --> 00:45:05.190
I can see it here.

00:45:05.190 --> 00:45:08.750
So you can also see a
more complicated analysis

00:45:08.750 --> 00:45:11.220
of estimation and
prediction analysis.

00:45:11.220 --> 00:45:19.120
Estimation is, again, a relative
risk of something happening

00:45:19.120 --> 00:45:20.540
based on your data set.

00:45:20.540 --> 00:45:23.830
And the more complicated
case just recently

00:45:23.830 --> 00:45:26.230
added is a patient level
prediction analysis.

00:45:26.230 --> 00:45:28.720
So patient level
prediction, basically,

00:45:28.720 --> 00:45:30.820
is the probability of
something happening

00:45:30.820 --> 00:45:33.040
for a very specific
type of patient,

00:45:33.040 --> 00:45:35.560
the probability of
developing certain conditions

00:45:35.560 --> 00:45:36.820
in the future.

00:45:36.820 --> 00:45:39.400
So patient level
prediction is just

00:45:39.400 --> 00:45:41.830
an example of, again, a
personalized treatment

00:45:41.830 --> 00:45:48.470
that is possible today to
develop for different patients.

00:45:48.470 --> 00:45:51.070
So I know I have
very limited time.

00:45:51.070 --> 00:45:54.610
And ATLAS, as you can see,
is a pretty extensive tool.

00:45:54.610 --> 00:45:57.170
Again, I'm really excited to
say that now ATLAS supports

00:45:57.170 --> 00:46:00.460
BigQuery as a database.

00:46:00.460 --> 00:46:03.580
And the last words, I would
like to say that, again,

00:46:03.580 --> 00:46:06.160
whatever you saw today,
ATLAS is an open source tool.

00:46:06.160 --> 00:46:07.300
So anybody can come.

00:46:07.300 --> 00:46:10.790
Anybody can join the community,
contribute, or use it.

00:46:10.790 --> 00:46:12.902
And I thank you
for your attention.

00:46:12.902 --> 00:46:13.860
MARIANNE SLIGHT: Great.

00:46:13.860 --> 00:46:15.442
Thank you very much, Greg.

00:46:15.442 --> 00:46:20.640
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:20.640 --> 00:46:23.740
So to wrap up--

00:46:23.740 --> 00:46:24.260
maybe.

00:46:24.260 --> 00:46:25.810
There we go.

00:46:25.810 --> 00:46:27.960
My closing message
for you, really,

00:46:27.960 --> 00:46:30.280
is start bringing your
health care and life science

00:46:30.280 --> 00:46:31.900
data onto Google Cloud.

00:46:31.900 --> 00:46:35.290
Start thinking about the
analytics that you want to do.

00:46:35.290 --> 00:46:37.930
Start thinking about
the harmonizations

00:46:37.930 --> 00:46:41.470
that you need to do and
where you want that data.

00:46:41.470 --> 00:46:44.890
Do you want it in
FHIR persisted on GCP,

00:46:44.890 --> 00:46:47.480
as Care Cloud has talked about?

00:46:47.480 --> 00:46:50.560
Do you want to bring it into
a specific target schema

00:46:50.560 --> 00:46:53.260
to enable collaboration
with others,

00:46:53.260 --> 00:46:55.330
as Greg has talked about?

00:46:55.330 --> 00:46:59.170
Most importantly, you
can get started now,

00:46:59.170 --> 00:47:02.170
but plan ahead for
scale, and think

00:47:02.170 --> 00:47:05.080
about how some of the
tools that we're building

00:47:05.080 --> 00:47:08.120
might help you do that
kind of data alignment.

00:47:08.120 --> 00:47:11.470
